<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    BUG-0148 follow-up, second occurrence (conexus relay 2026-07-27, T2 [21164]).

    THE MISS. rdr180-16-analyze-rewritten-tables adopted an explicit invariant
    on 2026-07-19: "a migration that rewrites a table ANALYZEs it in the same
    changelog pass." memory-002-1 and catalog-017-0 then rewrote two more
    tables — DROP INDEX -> DROP COLUMN fts_vector -> re-ADD the STORED
    generated column -> CREATE INDEX GIN — and neither ran ANALYZE. Re-adding
    a STORED generated column rewrites the table, so the invariant applied
    squarely and was simply not carried to the new changesets.

    MEASURED ON THE LIVE CLUSTER by conexus immediately after the v0.1.57 boot,
    against the changesets' own databasechangelog.dateexecuted (02:43:49Z):

        nexus.catalog_documents  n_live_tup=22133  newest stats 5757 min BEFORE the rewrite
        nexus.memory             n_live_tup=3459   newest stats  715 min BEFORE the rewrite

    They ran ANALYZE by hand before gating. This changeset makes that
    remediation part of the migration so no future boot depends on someone
    noticing.

    WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC. The precedent is a production outage shape, not a
    theory: at v0.1.48 a rewrite reset statistics, the stale-stats planner
    flipped hybrid queries off the GIN-bitmap plan onto the budget-bounded HNSW
    plan, and hybrid-search returned ZERO rows in production while /health,
    /version, the smoke round-trip AND the aggregate STEP-6 exit code all
    stayed green. A rewritten table also looks FRESH to autovacuum, so
    autoanalyze may never re-trigger on a read-mostly store — the stale stats
    can persist indefinitely rather than self-healing.

    It did not bite this time: nexus.memory and nexus.catalog_documents are
    small and off the hybrid path. That is luck about which tables were
    rewritten, not a property of the change, and it is invisible to every green
    signal we have.

    WHY A SEPARATE CHANGESET RATHER THAN AN EDIT. memory-002 and catalog-017
    have already executed — on the production cluster, on every dev box, and in
    CI. Editing them would break their Liquibase checksums. A new changeset in
    the same master pass is the only correct repair, and it is idempotent: on
    any store that already remediated by hand (the cloud did) it is a cheap
    re-stamp.

    ANALYZE is transaction-safe, RLS-exempt (maintenance, runs as the table
    owner), and takes no exclusive lock — it does not block readers or writers.

    THREE MORE FOUND BY THE LINT, not by inspection. Writing
    tests/test_changelog_analyze_lint.py to enforce the invariant immediately
    surfaced that catalog-015-0 (the 2026-07-13 separator fix on
    catalog_documents) and catalog-002-1-temporal-typing (an ALTER COLUMN TYPE
    on catalog_collections) had the SAME omission and had simply never been
    noticed. catalog-015-0 is covered by the catalog_documents ANALYZE below;
    catalog_collections needed its own line. So the invariant has now been
    missed four times across three authors — which is why it is mechanical now
    rather than a comment.

    NOT flagged, correctly: the *-baseline.xml changesets that add a generated
    column. Those run against a table created empty in the same pass, so there
    are no statistics to go stale and ANALYZE would be a no-op.

    FOR THE NEXT AUTHOR: if you write a changeset that rewrites a table, ANALYZE
    it in that same changeset rather than adding a line here. The lint accepts
    an ANALYZE anywhere at-or-after the rewrite in master order — that is what
    "the same changelog pass" means and it is what makes this file legal — but
    keeping the two together is what stops them drifting apart.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="analyze-002-0" author="nexus-22r1f">
        <comment>ANALYZE every table rewritten without one — memory-002-1, catalog-017-0, catalog-015-0 and catalog-002-1-temporal-typing (BUG-0148 invariant, conexus relay 2026-07-27)</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ANALYZE nexus.memory;
ANALYZE nexus.catalog_documents;
ANALYZE nexus.catalog_collections;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
